Article 4: The "mainly utilizing the material and technical conditions of a legal person or an unincorporated organization" as mentioned in Article 847, paragraph 2 of the Civil Code includes circumstances where, during the research and development process of the technical achievement, the employee utilizes all or most of the material conditions such as funds, equipment, apparatus or raw materials of the legal person or unincorporated organization, and such material conditions have a substantial impact on the formation of the technical achievement; it also includes circumstances where the substantive content of the technical achievement is completed on the basis of technical achievements or staged technical achievements not yet disclosed by the legal person or unincorporated organization. However, the following circumstances are excepted:
(1) where there is an agreement to return funds or pay usage fees for utilizing the material and technical conditions provided by the legal person or unincorporated organization;
(2) where the material and technical conditions of the legal person or unincorporated organization are utilized to verify or test the technical solution after the completion of the technical achievement.

Article 10: The following circumstances constitute the "illegal monopolization of technology" as mentioned in Article 850 of the Civil Code:
(1) restricting one party from conducting new research and development on the basis of the technology of the contract subject matter, or restricting its use of the improved technology, or where the conditions for the exchange of improved technology between the parties are unequal, including requiring one party to provide its independently improved technology to the other party free of charge, transfer it to the other party on a non-reciprocal basis, or exclusively own or share the intellectual property of the improved technology free of charge;
(2) restricting one party from obtaining, from other sources, technology similar to the technology provided by the technology provider or technology competing therewith;
(3) hindering one party from fully implementing the technology of the contract subject matter in a reasonable manner according to market demand, including obviously unreasonably restricting the quantity, variety, price, sales channels and export market of products produced or services provided by the technology recipient in implementing the technology of the contract subject matter;
(4) requiring the technology recipient to accept incidental conditions that are not essential for implementing the technology, including purchasing non-essential technology, raw materials, products, equipment, services, and accepting non-essential personnel, etc.;
(5) unreasonably restricting the channels or sources of the technology recipient's purchase of raw materials, components, products or equipment, etc.;
(6) prohibiting the technology recipient from raising objections to the validity of the intellectual property of the technology of the contract subject matter, or attaching conditions to raising objections.

Article 25 Patent exploitation licenses include the following forms:
(1) An exclusive exploitation license means that, within the scope of the patent exploitation licensed under the agreement, the licensor licenses only one licensee to exploit the patent, and the licensor shall not exploit the patent as agreed;
(2) A sole exploitation license means that, within the scope of the patent exploitation licensed under the agreement, the licensor licenses only one licensee to exploit the patent, but the licensor may exploit the patent itself as agreed;
(3) A non-exclusive exploitation license means that, within the scope of the patent exploitation licensed under the agreement, the licensor licenses another person to exploit the patent and may also exploit the patent itself.
Where the parties have not agreed on the form of patent exploitation license or the agreement is unclear, the license shall be determined as a non-exclusive exploitation license. Where a patent exploitation license contract stipulates that the licensee may sublicense another person to exploit the patent, the sublicense shall be determined as a non-exclusive exploitation license, unless the parties otherwise agree.
The form of licensing and use of technical secrets shall be determined by reference to the provisions of paragraphs 1 and 2 of this Article.
